### Case Summary ###
**Clinical Impression:**  
- **Suspected medial meniscus tear**, right or left knee (side not explicitly confirmed, but symptom localization implies symptomatic side is the one examined).  
- Mechanism: Non-contact twisting/pivoting during volleyball.  
- Key findings:  
  • Localized medial joint line tenderness (medial tibial plateau, just inferior to patella);  
  • Pain aggravated by rotational weight-bearing;  
  • No effusion, swelling, warmth, or erythema;  
  • Negative plain radiographs (no fracture, dislocation, or joint effusion).  

**Recommended Next Step:**  
✅ **MRI of the symptomatic knee**, with dedicated sequences for meniscal evaluation (e.g., proton-density weighted fat-saturated coronal and sagittal views), focusing on the **medial meniscus, medial tibial plateau, and patellofemoral joint**.  

**Not indicated at this time:**  
❌ Routine ultrasound (insufficient sensitivity/specificity for meniscal tear diagnosis);  
❌ Repeat X-ray (no new indication);  
❌ Immediate surgical referral — pending MRI results and functional assessment.  

**Interim Management Advice (to be provided to patient):**  
- Relative rest from pivoting/rotational sports;  
- Ice as needed for comfort;  
- NSAIDs if not contraindicated;  
- Avoid deep squatting, kneeling, or twisting under load;  
- Consider physical therapy evaluation for neuromuscular control and loading strategies — *pending MRI*.

